  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a relatively small sample of 435 respondents in a single city, which may limit the direct generalizability of the specific segment profiles to other urban contexts.",
      "what": "Based on a face-to-face survey of 435 car users in Hyderabad, India, this study uses benefit-based commuter segmentation and Meta Ranking to analyze how different groups perceive the advantages of congestion pricing. While travel-time minimization is the top perceived benefit overall, the analysis identifies four distinct commuter segments that prioritize different combinations of benefits.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that car users are not a homogeneous group in their expectations of congestion pricing, providing a segmentation methodology that allows planners to tailor policy design and communication strategies to specific user concerns before implementation."
    }
